  "slug": "fitness-dynamics-cb09",
  "summary": "New theory extends fitness landscape, epistasis and coexistence frameworks to dynamics across environments, population structure, space and mutation rate evolution.",
  "synthesis": "The items share a theoretical focus on how fitness is structured and how that structure shapes evolutionary trajectories. A review frames evolution as fitness landscape navigation, relating epistasis, ruggedness and accessibility. A global epistasis model fitted to yeast data predicts how non-home fitness changes during adaptation across backgrounds and environments. A multi-locus model finds that mutation rates evolve through indirect selection out of equilibrium and are selected against under homogenising selection. Two coexistence-theory preprints add within-population structure and competition-induced plasticity to niche and fitness differences. Host-parasite models show that spatial network topology and costs shape resistance and infectivity, and that parasite-impaired host mobility favours reduced virulence. The common move is from equilibrium fitness values toward context-dependent dynamics.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-24"
}
